<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">Br. J. Cancer</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Nature Publishing Group</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">10408836</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2362345</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">6690361</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/sj.bjc.6690361</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Regular Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Transient absorption changes in vivo during photodynamic therapy with pulsed-laser light</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Pogue</surname><given-names>B W</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Momma</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Wu</surname><given-names>H C</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Hasan</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><aff id="aff1">Wellman Laboratories of Photomedicine, Department of Dermatology, and Department of Pathology, Massachusetts General Hospital, Boston, MA 02114, USA</aff></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>5</month><year>1999</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>01</day><month>05</month><year>1999</year></pub-date><volume>80</volume><issue>3-4</issue><fpage>344</fpage><lpage>351</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>12</day><month>06</month><year>1998</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>04</day><month>11</month><year>1998</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>09</day><month>11</month><year>1998</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1999 Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-statement><copyright-year>1999</copyright-year><copyright-holder>Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-holder></permissions><abstract><p>High intensity pulsed-laser light can be used to excite absorbing molecules to transient states in large proportions. The laser-induced spectral changes can be characterized by transient changes in light propagation; through the tissue provided the excited states of these molecules have altered absorption spectra. Characterization of these transient changes may then be used to exploit new mechanisms in photosensitization and/or to optimize photobiological effects. In this study, transmittance and reflectance were measured as a function of laser pulse energy, from tissue-simulating media as well as in rat muscle and liver slices, both with and without the photosensitizer benzoporphyrin derivative monoacid (BPD-MA) present. There was a transient decrease in absorption from the photosensitizer at peak pulse irradiance in the range of 100&#x02013;1000 W cm<sup>&#x02013;2</sup>. The depth of photodynamic treatment-induced tissue necrosis was measured in a subcutaneous prostate cancer model in Copenhagen rats. A comparison between continuous wave irradiation and pulsed irradiation with the same average incident irradiance showed no statistically significant difference in the depth of necrosis at 48 h after irradiation. These results indicate that photosensitizer population-state changes are measurable in tissues and may provide a method for measuring triplet-state properties of photosensitizer in vivo, but for BPD-MA at clinically used concentrations these changes do not significantly affect the depth of photodynamically-induced tissue damage. &#x000a9; 1999 Cancer Research Campaign</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>photosensitizer</kwd><kwd>BPD-MA</kwd><kwd>pulsed-laser</kwd><kwd>optical dosimetry</kwd><kwd>tumour</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front></article>